.\nL675: \nL676: Artifacts provided. The bundle contains the tool; model generators and halo2-to-JSON extractor; R1CS corpus; prover/verifier sources; and drivers and results for every experiment in §【20†6】.\nL786: Our team has already identified 【218†the following issues†github.com】. We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL787: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a 【219†list of packages that need conversion†github.com】, and welcome 【220†developer contributions†github.com】.\nL788: \nL789: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【221†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view1\",\"pattern\":\"prime\"}); Total lines: 793\nL82: We borrow “sound” from ZK proof systems, where it forbids any prover from convincing the verifier of a false statement: a debloated constraint set $\\mathcal{C}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{C}$ is sound when every witness it accepts is also accepted by the original ($w\\models\\mathcal{C}^{\\prime}$ implies $w\\models\\mathcal{C}$); equivalently, every removed check $c$ is entailed by the survivors, i.e., a logical consequence of them ($\\mathcal{C}^{\\prime}\\vdash c$), leaving the model set unchanged.\nL85: Figure 1. Threat model: unsound debloating enables a forged proof being accepted by the verifier.A two-row flow diagram. A model enters a framework that produces circuit C, and a debloater transforms it into an unsoundly debloated circuit C prime. C prime is published to a malicious prover and a victim verifier. The prover creates a forged proof that satisfies C prime, causing the verifier to accept incorrectly.\nL278: Algorithm 1. Provenance graph construction.\nL279: \nL280: \nL281: \nL282: Input: Constraints $\\mathcal{C}$, fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$\nL283: \nL284: Output: Provenance graph $G=(F\\cup N,E)$\nL285: \nL286: 1:$F\\leftarrow\\{(v,k):v\\in\\mathcal{V},\\ k\\in\\mathcal{K},\\ \\sigma^{\\star}(v)\\Vdash(v,k)\\}$\nL287: \nL288: 2:$N\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL289: \nL290: 3:for each $c\\in\\mathcal{C}$ do\nL291: \nL292: 4: for each variable $v$ referenced by $c$ do\nL293: \nL294: 5:   $\\sigma^{\\prime}\\leftarrow\\sigma^{\\star}[v\\mapsto\\top]$ $\\triangleright$ reset $v$\nL295: 6:   $\\sigma^{\\prime\\prime}\\leftarrow\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma^{\\prime})$ $\\triangleright$ replay\nL296: \nL297: 7:   for each $k\\in\\mathcal{K}$ with $\\sigma^{\\prime\\prime}(v)\\Vdash(v,k)$ do\nL298: \nL299: 8:   $P\\leftarrow\\textsc{MinPremises}(c,v,k,\\sigma^{\\star})$\nL300: \nL301: 9:    $N\\leftarrow N\\cup\\{(c,\\ (v,k),\\ P)\\}$\nL302: \nL303: 10:$E\\leftarrow$ premise and derivation edges induced by $N$ (Def. 【80†4.1】)\nL304: \nL305: 11:return $(F\\cup N,\\ E)$\nL306: Algorithm for constructing the provenance graph. It iterates over constraints and referenced variables, resets one variable to top, replays the constraint transfer, minimizes the premises of every derived fact, adds the resulting constraint nodes, and returns the graph with premise and derivation edges.\nL307: Minimal premises. The replay above held every other-variable fact at $\\sigma^{\\star}$, but most were not actually consulted. Recording the maximal set would inflate the graph and, worse, manufacture spurious dependencies that the debloater would later have to mask around.\nL308: MinPremises (Alg. 【17†4.2】) trims them via a drop-to-top rule: candidates are individual facts $(v^{\\prime},k^{\\prime})$ about variables $v^{\\prime}$ that $c$ references, and the procedure tentatively drops each in turn (widening the affected variable toward $\\top$ via $\\mathit{state}(\\cdot)$ from Alg. 【17†4.2】), replays $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}$, and discards a candidate if the target still emerges. Soundness follows from §【16†4.1】’s monotonicity: a fact whose absence the target survives was not consulted.\nL309: Visit order is arbitrary; different orders yield different irreducible sets, but proof search needs some proof, not a minimum-cardinality one.\nL310: Algorithm 2. MinPremises via drop-to-top.\nL311: \nL312: \nL313: \nL314: Notation. For a fact set $S\\subseteq F$, let $\\mathit{state}(S)$ denote the abstract state assigning $v\\mapsto\\top$ and each other variable $u$ the narrowest abstract value consistent with the facts $\\{(u,k^{\\prime})\\in S\\}$.\nL315: \nL316: Input: Constraint $c$, target fact $(v,k)$, fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$\nL317: \nL318: Output: Irreducible $P\\subseteq F$ such that $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\mathit{state}(P))(v)\\Vdash(v,k)$\nL319: 1:$P\\leftarrow\\{(v^{\\prime},k^{\\prime}):v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathrm{vars}(c)\\setminus\\{v\\},\\ k^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{K},\\ \\sigma^{\\star}(v^{\\prime})\\Vdash(v^{\\prime},k^{\\prime})\\}$\nL320: \nL321: 2:for each $p\\in P$ (arbitrary order) do\nL322: \nL323: 3: $\\sigma^{\\prime}\\leftarrow\\mathit{state}(P\\setminus\\{p\\})$ $\\triangleright$ widen by dropping $p$\nL324: \nL325: 4: if $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma^{\\prime})(v)\\Vdash(v,k)$ then\nL326: \nL327: 5:  $P\\leftarrow P\\setminus\\{p\\}$ $\\triangleright$ $p$ unused\nL328: \nL329: 6:return $P$\nL330: Algorithm for minimizing the premises of a derived fact. Starting from all relevant fixpoint facts, it drops each premise in turn, reconstructs the abstract state, and permanently removes the premise when replaying the constraint still derives the target fact.\nL331: \nL332: Complexity. Write $n=|\\mathcal{C}|$ and $d$ for the maximum number of variables referenced by any single constraint.\nL333: ###### Theorem 4.2 (Construction complexity).\nL334: \nL335: Alg. 【17†4.2】 runs in time $O(n\\cdot d^{3})$ and produces a graph with $O(|\\mathcal{V}|)$ fact nodes, $O(n\\cdot d)$ constraint nodes, and $O(n\\cdot d^{2})$ edges.\nL336: \nL337: Usually, a constraint has a limited number of variables, so $d$ is a small constant, thus the construction is near-linear in $n$. The proof sketch appears in Appendix 【33†D】.\nL338: ### 4.3. Provenance-Guided Debloating\nL339: \nL340: Debloating selects constraints whose removal preserves the witness set. An entailment filter proposes candidates; a removal loop removes them one at a time, using AND/OR proof search on $G$ to certify each removal. We describe these components in order, then argue soundness.\nL341: Entailment filter. A constraint $c$ is a candidate, written $c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathsf{f}}$, when the fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ already implies its enforced property: $\\sigma^{\\star}\\Vdash_{\\mathsf{ent}}c$. We define $\\Vdash_{\\mathsf{ent}}$ by cases on the constraint shape; two are representative, and the remainder follow the same template.\nL342: \nL343:   * •\nL747: $w^{\\prime}$ agrees with the original witness $w$ on every other cell. The original rejects $w^{\\prime}$ by construction, since $\\ell$ is violated. Soundness requires that some constraint in $C_{\\mathit{deb}}$ also rejects $w^{\\prime}$; if every $C_{\\mathit{deb}}$ constraint accepts $w^{\\prime}$, the debloater has unsoundly trimmed $\\ell$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view1\",\"pattern\":\"interval\"}); Total lines: 793\nL224: ### 4.1. Abstract Interpretation over Constraints\nL225: \nL226: Abstract domain. Each variable $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$ is tracked by an abstract value drawn from a reduced product of three lattice domains:\nL227: \nL228:   1. (1)\nL229: \nL230: Constant: either a known field element or $\\top$ (unknown).\nL231: \nL232:   2. (2)\nL233: \nL234: Interval: a range $[\\ell,u]\\subseteq\\mathbb{Z}$ bounding all possible values of $v$ when interpreted as a signed integer.\nL235: \nL236:   3. (3)\nL237: Known bits: a bitmask $m$ and a value pattern $b$, recording that for every bit position $i$ where $m_{i}=1$, the $i$-th bit of $v$ equals $b_{i}$.\nL238: \nL239: The three components cross-propagate: a refinement in one domain sharpens the others via standard reduced-product rules (【54†Cousot and Cousot, 1977】; 【76†Cousot and Cousot, 1979】), as in LLVM’s KnownBits analysis (【77†The LLVM Project, 0000】).\nL240: Transfer functions. Each constraint type defines a transfer function that refines the abstract values of its variables. Transfer functions handle arithmetic gates (addition, multiplication, scaling, and their inverses), lookup tables (intersecting a variable’s abstract value with the table’s range), and bit decompositions (linking interval bounds to individual bit facts). Propagation is non-directional: $x+y=z$ may tighten any of $x$, $y$, or $z$ from the other two.\nL241: We show three representative rules in inference-rule form; the remainder follow the same template. Premises above the line are the abstract-state facts consulted; the conclusion below the line is the contributed refinement. $\\sigma$ denotes the current state; $A,B$ abbreviate $\\sigma(a),\\sigma(b)$; $\\sqsubseteq$ is the abstract-interval order (sharper bounds are smaller); and $A\\otimes B$ is the smallest interval containing $\\{ab:a\\in A,\\ b\\in B\\}$ (interval multiplication).\nL242:  | $$\\begin{array}[]{c}\\textsc{Lookup}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL243: \\dfrac{v\\in T\\hskip 9.24994pt\\mathrm{range}(T)=[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]}{[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)}\\end{array}\\hskip 18.49988pt\\begin{array}[]{c}\\textsc{Bitify}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL244: \\dfrac{\\begin{array}[]{c}v=\\textstyle\\sum_{i<n}b_{i}\\cdot 2^{i}\\hskip 9.24994ptb_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL245: \\sigma(b_{i})=\\{0\\}\\text{ for }i\\geq k\\end{array}}{[0,\\,2^{k}-1]\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)}\\end{array}$$  |\nL246:  | $$\\textsc{Quadratic gate}\\hskip 9.24994pt\\dfrac{a\\cdot b+c=0\\hskip 9.24994pt\\sigma(a)=A\\hskip 9.24994pt\\sigma(b)=B}{-(A\\otimes B)\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(c)}$$  |\nL247: Symmetric variants are elided: Bitify also propagates backward, and Quadratic gate solves backward for $a$ or $b$: rearranging $a\\cdot b+c=0$ divides by the other operand, giving $-(\\sigma(c)/\\sigma(b))\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(a)$ and symmetrically for $b$. The backward rule fires only when the divisor’s range is sign-determined (in particular, excludes zero), which makes the interval division well defined; otherwise it contributes nothing.\nL248: Soundness under field wrap-around. Witnesses live in $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$, not $\\mathbb{Z}$. We interpret a field element as its canonical signed representative in the window $W=[-(p-1)/2,\\,(p-1)/2]$.\nL249: A transfer rule is allowed to contribute an integer fact only when the concrete operation it abstracts is unique in this signed interpretation: for addition and scaling, the operand ranges and the resulting range must stay inside $W$; for multiplication, the full interval product must stay inside $W$; and for backward rules such as solving a quadratic gate for one operand, the divisor/range side conditions used by the rule must also keep the inferred range inside $W$.\nL250: Lookup and bit-decomposition rules are treated as integer facts only for table entries or bit widths whose represented values lie inside the same signed window. We enforce this discipline by widening any out-of-window conclusion to $\\top$:\nL251:  | $$\\textsc{WrapGuard}\\quad\\dfrac{[\\ell,u]\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)\\;\\text{(derived above)}\\quad[\\ell,u]\\not\\subseteq W}{\\top\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)}$$  |\nL252: \nL253: Thus, any modular wrap-around that would make the integer reading ambiguous causes the analysis to keep no refinement from that rule. With the wrap-around guard, the analysis performs a refinement only when integer and modular arithmetic agree on it, so every kept fact holds for every witness (wrap-around or not).\nL254: Fixpoint computation. Variables start at $\\top$, except those pinned to circuit constants (e.g., fixed-column entries or hard-coded values), which start at the corresponding constant. A standard worklist re-applies each constraint’s transfer function whenever one of its variables is refined.\nL255: Soundness, termination, and complexity. Each transfer function is locally sound: for every constraint $c$ and every witness $w\\models c$, applying $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}$ to any abstract state that over-approximates $w$ yields a state that still over-approximates $w$. We verify this condition per constraint type and take it as a precondition on the rules.\nL256: Local soundness, monotonicity ($\\sigma_{1}\\sqsubseteq\\sigma_{2}\\Rightarrow\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma_{1})\\sqsubseteq\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma_{2})$), and the bounded height of the lattice (every interval lies in $W$ by WrapGuard) are the standard ingredients of the worklist fixpoint construction (【54†Cousot and Cousot, 1977】; 【76†Cousot and Cousot, 1979】): chaotic iteration (a worklist with arbitrary dequeue order) reaches a fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ that over-approximates every $w\\models\\mathcal{C}$.\nL257: With $n=|\\mathcal{C}|$ constraints, $d$ the maximum constraint arity, and $h$ the height of the interval lattice over $W$, the construction runs in $O(n\\cdot d\\cdot h)$ time.\nL258: ### 4.2. Provenance Graph\nL259: The fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ proves facts but discards how they were derived; the premise asymmetry of Challenge B shows why that history matters. To handle it without re-running the fixpoint after every candidate removal, we record the derivation structure as a provenance graph (【78†Doyle, 1979】; 【79†de Kleer, 1986】): a bipartite AND/OR graph whose fact nodes (OR) hold proven facts and whose constraint nodes (AND) record which constraints, with which premise facts, established them.\nL260: To track derivations at the granularity downstream entailment needs, we project each variable’s reduced-product value $\\sigma^{\\star}(v)$ into up to three atomic facts, one per abstract domain: a lower bound, an upper bound, and a known-bits pattern. Each atom is separately provable, so a constraint can take credit for the specific atom it contributes and provenance tracks each atom on its own.\nL261: For a state $\\sigma$ and a fact $k$ over variable $v$, we write $\\sigma(v)\\Vdash k$ to mean that every concrete value in $\\gamma(\\sigma(v))$ satisfies $k$. We use $\\Vdash$ to distinguish abstract from semantic entailment (§【12†3.1】).\nL262: ###### Definition 0 (Provenance graph).\nL263: \nL264: The provenance graph of constraint system $\\mathcal{C}$ at fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ is a directed bipartite graph $G=(F\\cup N,\\,E)$ with parts $F$ (fact nodes, the OR side) and $N$ (constraint nodes, the AND side):\nL265: \nL266:   * •\nL564: Incompleteness. Exact maximum debloating is intractable at our target scale (Challenge C, §【14†3.3】). Our sound but incomplete analysis uses interval, known-bits, and constant domains, template-based entailment rules, and greedy removal. These polynomial-time approximations may miss redundant checks, but every removal remains justified by provenance-guided proof search over the surviving constraints.\nL709: We trace interval facts through five constraints distilled from §【13†3.2】: $c_{\\mathsf{relu}}:z\\in[0,10]$, $c_{\\mathsf{rng}}:z\\in[0,5]$, $c_{\\mathsf{sum}}:x+y=10$, and $c_{x}:x\\in[0,10]$, $c_{y}:y\\in[0,10]$. The $\\{c_{\\mathsf{relu}},c_{\\mathsf{rng}}\\}$ and $\\{c_{\\mathsf{sum}},c_{x},c_{y}\\}$ subsystems share no variables.\nL718: ### F.1. Range subsumption (zkml/MNIST-MLP)\nL719: \nL720: Of the $791$ removed constraints, $788$ are lookups and $3$ are gates. Each removed lookup is a range proof on a variable whose range is already pinned to an at-least-as-tight interval by some other constraint in the circuit. Three patterns recur on this benchmark and account for nearly all $788$ lookup trims (Fig. 【211†5】).\nL721: Figure 5. Three range-subsumption patterns. Dashed boxes are the redundant lookups.Three patterns: a ReLU bound subsumes a wider range lookup; a ReLU bound subsumes a shifted Packer lookup; and a fixed divisor together with a two-input lookup entails a remainder lookup. Dashed boxes mark removals.\nL722: Pattern (a). The ReLU gadget contains a lookup table that, by construction, pins its output variable $\\mathit{act}$ to $[0,128]$. Further downstream, the compiler emits a separate, generic range check on the same variable against the wider interval $[0,256]$. The first lookup already implies the second, so the second is removable.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view1\",\"pattern\":\"Open Science\"}); Total lines: 793\nL15:   1. 【5†Abstract.】\nL16:   2. 【6†1 Introduction】\nL17:   3. 【7†2 Background】\nL18:     1. 【8†2.1 Constraints in ZK Proof Systems】\nL19:     2. 【9†2.2 ZK Machine Learning Frameworks】\nL20:     3. 【10†2.3 Constraint Optimization in ZK Systems】\nL21:   4. 【11†3 Problem and Motivation】\nL22:     1. 【12†3.1 Problem Formulation】\nL23:     2. 【13†3.2 Motivating Example】\nL24:     3. 【14†3.3 Design Challenges】\nL25:   5. 【15†4 Approach】\nL26:     1. 【16†4.1 Abstract Interpretation over Constraints】\nL27:     2. 【17†4.2 Provenance Graph】\nL28:     3. 【18†4.3 Provenance-Guided Debloating】\nL29:   6. 【19†5 Implementation】\nL30:   7. 【20†6 Evaluation】\nL31:     1. 【21†6.1 Experimental Setup】\nL32:     2. 【22†6.2 RQ1: Effectiveness】\nL33:     3. 【23†6.3 RQ2: Scalability】\nL34:     4. 【24†6.4 RQ3: Comparison】\nL35:   8. 【25†7 Discussion】\nL36:   9. 【26†8 Related Work】\nL37:   10. 【27†9 Conclusion】\nL38:     1. 【28†Acknowledgements】\nL39:   11. 【29†References】\nL40:   12. 【30†A Open Science】\nL41:   13. 【31†B Ethical Considerations】\nL42:   14. 【32†C Generative AI Usage】\nL43:   15. 【33†D Proof Sketches】\nL44:   16. 【34†E Illustrative Walkthrough】\nL45:   17. 【35†F Case Studies of Removed Constraints】\nL46:     1. 【36†F.1 Range subsumption (zkml/MNIST-MLP)】\nL47:     2. 【37†F.2 Activation-bound cascade (ezkl/BERT-tiny)】\nL48:     3. 【38†F.3 Recurrent amplification (zkml/PTB-LSTM)】\nL49:   18. 【39†G Empirical Soundness Validation】\nL50:     1. 【40†G.1 Test design】\nL51:     2. 【41†G.2 Results】\nL52: 【42†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org】\nL53: \nL54: arXiv:2609.10149v1 [cs.CR] 09 Sep 2026\nL55: # Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits Note: This is the authors’ version for ACM CCS 2026, with additional appendices. Conference version: Zhantong Xue, Pingchuan Ma, Zhaoyu Wang, Yuguang Zhou, Huaijin Wang, and Shuai Wang. 2026. Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits. In Proceedings of the 2026 ACM SIGSAC Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS ’26), November 15–19, 2026, The Hague, Netherlands. ACM.\nL671:   * Zhang et al. (2021) Jiang Zhang, Shuai Wang, Manuel Rigger, Pinjia He, and Zhendong Su. 2021. SANRAZOR: Reducing Redundant Sanitizer Checks in C/C++ Programs. In 15th USENIX Symposium on Operating Systems Design and Implementation (OSDI 21). USENIX Association, 479–494. 【206†https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi21/presentation/zhang†www.usenix.org】\nL672: ## Appendix A Open Science\nL673: \nL674: Our complete Rust implementation and evaluation artifact are available at 【207†https://github.com/xuezhantong/zk-debloater†github.com】.\nL675: \nL676: Artifacts provided. The bundle contains the tool; model generators and halo2-to-JSON extractor; R1CS corpus; prover/verifier sources; and drivers and results for every experiment in §【20†6】.\nL677: ## Appendix B Ethical Considerations\nL678: \nL679: This work involves no human subjects, personal data, or vulnerability disclosure. Its static analysis removes only provably redundant constraints; we foresee no negative ethical implications.\nL680: \nL681: ## Appendix C Generative AI Usage\nL682: \nL683: We used OpenAI GPT and Anthropic Claude for proofreading, rephrasing, formatting, debugging, and implementation suggestions. The authors reviewed and verified all generated content and code for accuracy, correctness, and originality.\nL684: ## Appendix D Proof Sketches\nL685: \nL686: We sketch the complexity theorem of §【17†4.2】 and the soundness theorems of §【18†4.3】.\nL687: \nL688: ###### Proof sketch of Theorem 【208†4.2】.\nL689: \nL690: Each $(c,v)$ pair uses one $O(d)$ transfer replay and $O(d)$ premise trials of $O(d)$ each, hence $O(d^{2})$ per pair and $O(nd^{3})$ overall. The graph bounds follow from $|\\mathcal{K}|=3$ and at most $d-1$ premises per constraint node. ∎\n"
